## Spring Data JPA Projections — Documentation Summary

**Context7 library ID used:** `/spring-projects/spring-data-jpa`
**Source reputation:** High | **Benchmark score:** 77.26

---

## Overview

Spring Data JPA supports several kinds of projections that let you retrieve a subset of an entity's attributes instead of the full entity graph. The four main categories are:

1. Interface-based projections (closed)
2. Interface-based projections (open — with SpEL)
3. Class-based (DTO) projections
4. Dynamic projections

---

## 1. Interface-based Projections — Closed

A **closed projection** is an interface whose accessor methods match property names of the target entity exactly. Spring Data JPA can optimise the query to select only those columns.

```java
// Domain entity
class User {
    Long id;
    String firstname;
    String lastname;
    Address address;
}

// Closed projection — only firstname and lastname
interface NamesOnly {
    String getFirstname();
    String getLastname();
}
```

Repository usage:

```java
interface UserRepository extends Repository<User, Long> {
    List<NamesOnly> findByLastname(String lastname);
}
```

Spring Data JPA uses `Tuple` queries internally to construct interface proxies for interface-based projections. Query derivation supports both class-based and interface projections by introspecting the returned type.

> **Caveat:** Projections limit the selection to top-level properties of the target entity. Any nested properties that resolve to joins select the **entire** nested property — the full join is materialised even if you only access one field of it.

---

## 2. Interface-based Projections — Open (SpEL)

An **open projection** uses `@Value` with a SpEL expression on the accessor method. Because the expression can reference `target` (the backing entity), JPA cannot optimise the query — the full entity is loaded.

```java
interface NamesOnly {

    @Value("#{target.firstname + ' ' + target.lastname}")
    String getFullName();
}
```

You can also delegate the computation to a default method to keep the projection interface cleaner:

```java
interface NamesOnly {

    String getFirstname();
    String getLastname();

    default String getFullName() {
        return getFirstname().concat(" ").concat(getLastname());
    }
}
```

Or, with a backing bean:

```java
@Component
class MyBean {
    String getFullName(User user) {
        return ...;
    }
}

interface NamesOnly {
    @Value("#{@myBean.getFullName(target)}")
    String getFullName();
}
```

---

## 3. Class-based (DTO) Projections

Class-based projections use concrete classes (plain classes, Lombok `@Value`, or Java records) with an all-args constructor. Spring Data JPA uses JPA's constructor expression mechanism (`SELECT new …`) to populate them — no proxies involved.

### Derived queries

```java
// DTO / record
record NamesOnly(String firstname, String lastname) {}

// Repository
interface UserRepository extends Repository<User, Long> {
    List<NamesOnly> findByLastname(String lastname);
}
```

Spring Data JPA introspects the return type and rewrites the derived query to use `SELECT new com.example.NamesOnly(u.firstname, u.lastname) FROM User u WHERE …` under the hood.

### JPQL Queries — `@Query`

For `@Query`-annotated methods, Spring Data JPA can rewrite the query automatically if it selects the root object or individual properties. The rewriting is **bypassed** if the query already contains a constructor expression.

```java
interface UserRepository extends Repository<User, Long> {

    // JPA rewrites this — selects root entity → constructor expression
    @Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.lastname = :lastname")
    List<UserDto> findByLastname(String lastname);

    // JPA rewrites this — selects individual columns → constructor expression
    @Query("SELECT u.firstname, u.lastname FROM User u WHERE u.lastname = :lastname")
    List<UserDto> findMultipleColumnsByLastname(String lastname);
}

record UserDto(String firstname, String lastname) {}
```

If you write the constructor expression manually:

```java
@Query("SELECT new com.example.NamesOnly(u.firstname, u.lastname) FROM User u")
List<NamesOnly> findAllProjectedBy();
```

### Aggregate / grouped DTO projection

```java
@UserRoleCountProjectingQuery
List<UserRoleCountDtoProjection> dtoProjectionEntityAndAggregatedValue();

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Query("select u, count(r) from User u left outer join u.roles r group by u")
@interface UserRoleCountProjectingQuery {
}
```

### Native Queries

When using class-based projections with native queries:

- If result column names map **directly** to the DTO's constructor arguments (same order and compatible types), you can simply declare the DTO as the return type.
- If column names don't match or require transformation, use `@SqlResultSetMapping` and reference the mapping name in `@NativeQuery`.

> **Requirement:** The DTO must have an all-args constructor regardless of the query style.

---

## 4. Dynamic Projections

Dynamic projections let the caller choose the projection type at call time via a generic type parameter. Spring Data JPA resolves the actual projection at runtime.

```java
interface UserRepository extends Repository<User, Long> {
    <T> List<T> findByLastname(String lastname, Class<T> type);
}
```

Usage:

```java
// Return full entities
List<User> users = repository.findByLastname("Matthews", User.class);

// Return interface-based projection
List<NamesOnly> names = repository.findByLastname("Matthews", NamesOnly.class);

// Return DTO projection
List<NamesOnly> dtos = repository.findByLastname("Matthews", UserDto.class);
```

This allows a single repository method to cover multiple output shapes without duplication.

---

## Summary Table

| Type | Mechanism | Query optimised? | Proxy? | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Closed interface | Interface with matching getters | Yes (Tuple query) | Yes | Full join materialised for nested props |
| Open interface (SpEL) | `@Value` SpEL on getter | No (full entity loaded) | Yes | Can delegate to default method or bean |
| Class-based (DTO) | All-args constructor / record | Yes (constructor expr) | No | JPQL rewriting unless constructor expr already present |
| Dynamic | Generic `Class<T>` param | Depends on `T` | Depends on `T` | Single method, multiple shapes |

---

## AOT (Ahead-of-Time) Support

Both interface projections and DTO projections are supported in Spring Data JPA's AOT (native image) mode, alongside derived queries, `@Query`/`@NativeQuery`, pagination, `Slice`, `Stream`, and `Optional` return types.

